crypto/tls: add Config.KeyExchanges for external key exchange groups

The set of (EC)DHE groups in crypto/tls is closed, and unknown
CurvePreferences entries are ignored. Products in regulated
environments must offer groups that crypto/tls doesn't implement: TLS
peers in the German health care network must support brainpoolP256r1
and brainpoolP384r1 (RFC 7027), and OpenSSL servers refuse a client's
Brainpool certificate unless the client lists a Brainpool group in
supported_groups.

Add a KeyExchange type and Config.KeyExchanges. A KeyExchange is a named
group implemented outside crypto/tls, modeled with crypto.Encapsulator
and crypto.Decapsulator, the interfaces crypto/tls already uses for its
ML-KEM key shares (key_schedule.go). External groups are offered and
accepted after the built-in groups, in slice order; CurvePreferences
filters them like built-in groups. MinVersion and MaxVersion scope an
entry to protocol versions, because some groups have different IDs in
TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 (RFC 7027, RFC 8734). Each handshake generates a
fresh key. An entry with the ID of a built-in group, or two entries
with the same ID for overlapping versions, are an error. External
groups are never used in FIPS 140-3 mode.

Unlike the custom key exchanges declined in golang/go#31520, this adds
no key exchange method and no cipher suite: only named groups inside the
existing ECDHE (and, later, TLS 1.3 key share) flows. It exposes no
internals of crypto/tls, and it serves regulated production use, not
experimentation. If an exported API is not acceptable, a GOEXPERIMENT
gate is a possible fallback.

In TLS 1.3, external groups are key share groups, modeled the same way
as ML-KEM: the client generates the key and the server encapsulates. A
client sends a key share for its first group, which is an external
group when CurvePreferences lists only external groups (as for servers
that accept only Brainpool groups, RFC 8734), and a server may select an
external group in a HelloRetryRequest. A client that offers TLS 1.2 and
TLS 1.3 lists the groups limited to TLS 1.2 after the others; if it has
no group for TLS 1.3, it offers only TLS 1.2 (Config.supportedVersions,
so that the downgrade check agrees), instead of failing with "no
supported key exchange methods". A HelloRetryRequest that selects a
group the client offered only for TLS 1.2 draws illegal_parameter. An
invalid key share fails as for the built-in groups.

The tests wrap crypto/ecdh's P-256 and X25519 as KEMs under private-use
group IDs (RFC 8446 reserves 0xFE00-0xFEFF).

ivory: P4, profiles core and kt. Base: go1.27.2 with P0-P3.
Upstream: proposal C. Requirements: GS-A_4384-03, A_17094-02, A_23912,
A_23913 (gemSpec_Krypt V2.51.0).
Status: TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3.

